    {
      "type": "vo",
      "name": "real_vulnerability",
      "text": "There is a real reason to pause before calling this inevitable. Sevilla's first-half pressure made Barcelona work, and the home side scored first. Levante nearly turned a three-goal deficit into a result late in their match with Barcelona. This team can lose control for spells; a stronger European opponent may punish those spells more severely. In the Sevilla studio discussion, the defensive transition question was raised for exactly that reason. But notice what the comeback also proved. Barcelona did not need a comfortable game to win it. Raphinha's movement gave them a route back, and Yamal's deliveries gave them two more ways through. The weakness matters. It does not erase the attacking question. It makes it sharper: can an opponent pressure Barcelona for ninety minutes while also tracking all the ways this new front line can hurt them? That is the honest version of the story. A perfect start proves Barcelona can keep winning; it does not prove no one can exploit their open spaces. The coming matches will tell us how well they manage that trade-off.",
